                        "sort_order": 6,
                        "title": "One platform instead of ten subscriptions",
                        "subtitle": "Why teams consolidate their AI tooling here.",
                        "content": "<p>Most teams arrive with a drawer full of half-used AI subscriptions: one for copy, another for images, a third for transcripts, and a spreadsheet somewhere tracking who pays for what. Every one of them bills separately, enforces its own limits, and reports usage in its own format.</p><p>Running them from a single platform changes the arithmetic. Credits are shared across every tool, so a quiet month on images pays for a busy month on writing. Access follows the plan a member is on rather than a pile of seat licences. And because usage is recorded in one place, the question <em>“what did AI actually cost us last month?”</em> has an answer you can export.</p><p>Start on the free tier, connect your own provider keys when volume justifies it, and grow into the paid plans only when the credits run short.</p>",
                        "badge_text": "Why consolidate",
